{"records":[{"id":"8bck675bht","title":"Identification of ICAT as an APC inhibitor, revealing Wnt-dependent inhibition of APC-Axin interaction","description":"Adenomatous polyposis coli (APC) and Axin are core components of the beta-catenin destruction complex. How APC’s function is regulated and whether Wnt signaling influences the direct APC-Axin interaction to inhibit the beta-catenin destruction complex and not clear. Through a CRISPR screen of beta-catenin stability, we have identified ICAT, a polypeptide previously known to block beta-catenin-TCF interaction, as a natural inhibitor of APC.  ICAT blocks beta-catenin-APC interaction and prevents beta-catenin-mediated APC-Axin interaction, enhancing stabilization of beta-catenin in cells harboring truncated APC or stimulated with Wnt, but not in cells deprived of a Wnt signal. Using ICAT as a tool to disengage beta-catenin-mediated APC-Axin interaction, we demonstrate that Wnt quickly inhibits the direct interaction between APC and Axin. Our study highlights an important scaffolding function of beta-catenin in the assembly of the destruction complex and suggests Wnt-inhibited APC-Axin interaction as a novel mechanism of Wnt-dependent inhibition of the destruction complex.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2018-09-14T18:33:43.875Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/8bck675bht","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/8bck675bht"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Feng","last_name":"Cong","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Lei","last_name":"Ji","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Bo","last_name":"Lu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Zhizhi","last_name":"Wang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Zinger","last_name":"Yang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"John","last_name":"Reece-Hoyes","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Carsten","last_name":"Russ","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Wenqing","last_name":"Xu","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Molecular Biology","Cell Biology"],"journal_issn":["10972765"]},{"id":"477bs297n5","title":"A Study of PKCα-mediated nuclear translocation of cGAS stabilizes β-catenin and drives metastasis. Qimin Zhang et al","description":"Although recent studies have shown that 2′3′-cyclic GMP-AMP synthase (cGAS) has both canonical cytoplasmic and noncanonical nuclear functions, the role of nuclear cGAS in cancer metastasis remains unclear. Here, we identify a STING-independent mechanism by which nuclear cGAS activates Wnt/β-catenin signaling to promote metastatic dissemination in triple-negative breast cancer (TNBC). Cytosolic protein kinase C alpha (PKCα) phosphorylates cGAS at Ser120, facilitating its nuclear translocation. Once in the nucleus, cGAS disrupts the interaction between β-catenin and tripartite motif-containing protein 33 (TRIM33), an E3 ubiquitin ligase, thereby preventing β-catenin ubiquitination and promoting its stabilization. This, in turn, activates Wnt/β-catenin signaling. PKCα thus acts as a key regulator of this non-canonical, cGAS-driven metastatic pathway. Therapeutic TAT peptides designed to inhibit cGAS phosphorylation significantly reduce metastatic dissemination. Clinically, elevated nuclear cGAS expression is associated with increased metastasis in TNBC cohorts. Together, these findings delineate a PKCα–cGAS–TRIM33 axis that regulates nuclear β-catenin stability and establish cGAS phosphorylation as a promising theragnostic target for TNBC metastasis.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-06-22T09:35:51.176Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/477bs297n5","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/477bs297n5"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Qimin","last_name":"Zhang","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","grant_id":"82172823, 32450566, and 81901621"},{"id":"https://ror.org/04323m874","name":"Science and Technology Department of Sichuan Province","grant_id":"2024NSFJQ0047"},{"id":"https://ror.org/0426zh255","name":"China Postdoctoral Science Foundation","grant_id":"2019M652624"}],"categories":["Metastasis","Ubiquitylation","Triple-Negative Breast Cancer","cGAS-STING Signaling Pathway"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"dfm3zy7h8z","title":"Targeting KRT17 inhibits ovarian cancer progression and platinum resistance by suppressing cancer stemness through the EPN1/Wnt/β -catenin axis----PART1","description":"Data for paper “KRT17 Promotes Ovarian Cancer Stemness，Progression, and Resistance to Platinum via Stabilization of EPN1 and Activation of the Wnt/β-catenin Pathway”---PARTI","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-01-05T13:18:29.517Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/dfm3zy7h8z","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/dfm3zy7h8z"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"G","last_name":"du","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Biochemistry","Cell Biology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"gbjy9dhyct","title":"Hippocampal Wnt/β-catenin signaling pathway inhibition exacerbates postoperative neurocognitive disorders in diabetic-aged mice","description":"Original western blot for the article \"Hippocampal Wnt/β-catenin signaling pathway inhibition exacerbates postoperative neurocognitive disorders in diabetic-aged mice\"","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-01-02T08:26:52.305Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/gbjy9dhyct","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/gbjy9dhyct"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Danfeng","last_name":"Wang","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Western Blot"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"z5s9xnkx6n","title":"Original WB Li and Kang et. al. Developmental Cell","description":"The neurotransmitter GABA has been thought to be involved in the development of some types of cancer. Yet, the de novo synthesis of GABA and how it functions in hepatocellular carcinoma (HCC) remain unclear. Here, we report that SLC6A12 acts as a transporter of GABA and ALDH9A1 (aldehyde dehydrogenase 9 family member A1), not GAD1 (glutamate decarboxylase 1), generates GABA in human HCC. Interestingly, SLC6A12 and ALDH9A1 are upregulated during lung metastases of HCC, and depletion of either of them leads to impaired HCC metastasis. Mechanistically, GABA directly binds and stabilizes β-catenin, resulting in activated Wnt/β-catenin signaling, and thereby enhanced HCC metastasis. Reciprocally, β-catenin transcriptionally upregulates SLC6A12 to import more GABA to stabilize β-catenin. Thus, our findings identify ALDH9A1 is the major GABA synthetase in HCC, demonstrate a positive-feedback regulatory mechanism for sustaining Wnt/β-catenin signaling, and find a role for β-catenin in sensing GABA that contributes to HCC metastasis.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-11-26T18:16:58.604Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/z5s9xnkx6n","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/z5s9xnkx6n"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Li","last_name":"Li","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Western Blot"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"r7tcjmb3nt","title":"Targeting KRT17 inhibits ovarian cancer progression and platinum resistance by suppressing cancer stemness through the EPN1/Wnt/β -catenin axis------ PARTII","description":"Paper \"KRT17 Promotes Ovarian Cancer Stemness\" Progression,  the second part of the original data of \"and Resistance to Platinum via Stabilization of EPN1 and Activation of the Wnt/β-catenin Pathway\" is the original data of animal experiments-PARTII","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-01-05T13:18:33.452Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/r7tcjmb3nt","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/r7tcjmb3nt"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"G","last_name":"du","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Biochemistry"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"jvpgmxs3n6","title":"Supplemental material for: \"A genome-wide association meta-analysis links hidradenitis suppurativa to common and rare sequence variants causing disruption of the Notch and Wnt/β-catenin signaling pathways\"","description":"Supplemental material for the manuscript \"A genome-wide association meta-analysis links hidradenitis suppurativa to common and rare sequence variants causing disruption of the Notch and Wnt/β-catenin signaling pathways\"","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-01-31T13:10:54.026Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/jvpgmxs3n6","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/jvpgmxs3n6"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Rune","last_name":"KjÃ¦rsgaard Andersen","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Lilja","last_name":"Stefansdottir","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Peter","last_name":"Riis","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Gisli","last_name":"Halldorsson","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Egil","last_name":"Ferkingstad","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Asmundur","last_name":"Oddsson","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Bragi","last_name":"Walters","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Thorunn","last_name":"Olafsdottir","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Gudrun","last_name":"Rutsdottir","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Claus","last_name":"Zachariae","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Simon","last_name":"Thomsen","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Thorsten","last_name":"Brodersen","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Khoa","last_name":"Dinh","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Kirk","last_name":"Knowlton","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Stacey","last_name":"Knight","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Lincoln","last_name":"Nadauld","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Karina","last_name":"Banasik","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Søren","last_name":"Brunak","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Thomas","last_name":"Hansen","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Henerik","last_name":"Hjalgrim","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Erik","last_name":"Sørensen","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Christina","last_name":"Mikkelsen","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Henrik","last_name":"Ullum","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Mette","last_name":"Nyegaard","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Mie","last_name":"Brunn","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Christian","last_name":"Erikstrup","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Sisse","last_name":"Ostrowski","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Liv","last_name":"Eidsmo","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Ditte","last_name":"Saunte","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Bardur","last_name":"Sigurgeirsson","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Kjartar","last_name":"Ovar","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jona","last_name":"Saemundsdottir","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Pall","last_name":"Melsted","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Gudmundur","last_name":"Norddahl","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Patrick","last_name":"Sulem","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Hreinn","last_name":"Stefansson","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Hilma","last_name":"Holm","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Daniel","last_name":"Gudbjartsson","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Gudmar","last_name":"Thorleifsson","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Ingileif","last_name":"Jonsdottir","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Ole","last_name":"Pedersen","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Gregor","last_name":"Jemec","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Kari","last_name":"Stefansson","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/02hcraz70","name":"Aase og Ejnar Danielsens Fond","grant_id":null},{"id":"https://ror.org/02rgsr590","name":"LEO Foundation","grant_id":"LF 18002"},{"id":"https://ror.org/04txyc737","name":"Novo Nordisk Foundation","grant_id":"NNF20SA0064340 "}],"categories":["Genetics","Dermatology","Genome Wide Association Study","Hidradenitis Suppurativa"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"fwh957stfy","title":"Retinoic acid-related orphan nuclear receptor alpha promotes cartilage degeneration in osteoarthritis by activating the Wnt/β-catenin pathway","description":"This dataset contains transcriptomic sequencing data generated for the study “Retinoic acid-related orphan nuclear receptor alpha promotes cartilage degeneration in osteoarthritis by activating the Wnt/β-catenin pathway.”\nThe dataset includes RNA-seq data from primary mouse chondrocytes under control and osteoarthritis-like conditions, as well as RNA-seq data from articular cartilage tissues isolated from chondrocyte-specific RORα conditional knockout mice and wild-type littermate controls. These data were used to identify differentially expressed genes and enriched biological pathways associated with osteoarthritis-like chondrocyte degeneration and RORα deficiency.\nDifferential expression analysis was performed to explore the downstream regulatory mechanisms of RORα in cartilage degeneration. The results supported the involvement of Wnt/β-catenin-related transcriptional programs and cholesterol/lipid metabolism-associated genes in RORα-mediated regulation of cartilage extracellular matrix metabolism. These data provide supporting evidence for the conclusion that RORα is functionally linked to β-catenin/Wnt signaling and contributes to extracellular matrix degradation during osteoarthritis progression.\nThe dataset is provided to support transparency, reproducibility, and further reuse of the transcriptomic analyses reported in the associated manuscript.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-05-01T11:27:31.935Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/fwh957stfy","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/fwh957stfy"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Rui-Jue","last_name":"Zhu","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Clinical Medicine"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"2v5jg2zksj","title":"TMEM79/MATTRIN defines a pathway for Frizzled regulation and is required for Xenopus embryogenesis","description":"Absctract\nWnt signaling through the Frizzled (FZD) family of serpentine receptors is essential for embryogenesis and homeostasis, and stringent control of the FZD protein level is critical for stem cell regulation. Through CRISPR/Cas9 genome-wide screening in human cells, we identified TMEM79/MATTRIN, an orphan multi-span transmembrane protein, as a specific inhibitor of Wnt/FZD signaling. TMEM79 interacts with FZD during biogenesis and promotes FZD degradation independent of ZNRF3/RNF43 ubiquitin ligases (R-spondin receptors). TMEM79 interacts with ubiquitin-specific protease 8 (USP8), whose activating mutations underlie human tumorigenesis. TMEM79 specifically inhibits USP8 deubiquitination of FZD, thereby governing USP8 substrate specificity and promoting FZD degradation. Tmem79 and Usp8 genes have a pre-bilaterian origin, and Tmem79 inhibition of Usp8 and Wnt signaling is required for anterior neural development and gastrulation in Xenopus embryos. TMEM79 is a predisposition gene for Atopic dermatitis, suggesting deregulation of Wnt/FZD signaling a possible cause for this most common yet enigmatic inflammatory skin disease.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-09-26T05:47:58.606Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/2v5jg2zksj","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/2v5jg2zksj"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Maorong","last_name":"Chen","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Nathalia","last_name":"Amado","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jieqiong","last_name":"Tan","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Alice ","last_name":"Reis","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Mengxu","last_name":"Ge","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jose","last_name":"Abreu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Xi","last_name":"He","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Biochemistry","Molecular Biology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"99bt56v4zs","title":"Mediator condensates localize signaling factors to key cell identity genes. Zamudio et al. S1","description":"The gene expression programs that define each cell’s identity are controlled by master transcription factors (TFs) that bind cell-type specific enhancers, as well as signaling factors, which bring extracellular stimuli to these enhancers. Recent studies have revealed that master TFs form phase-separated condensates with the Mediator coactivator at super-enhancers. Here we present evidence that signaling factors for the WNT, TGF-β and JAK/STAT pathways employ their intrinsically disordered regions (IDRs) to enter and concentrate in Mediator condensates at super-enhancers. We show that the WNT coactivator β-catenin interacts both with components of condensates and DNA binding factors to selectively occupy super-enhancer associated genes. We propose that the cell-type specificity of the response to signaling is mediated, in part, by the IDRs of the signaling factors, which cause these factors to partition into condensates established by the master TFs and Mediator at genes with prominent roles in cell identity.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2019-09-16T17:58:57.347Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/99bt56v4zs","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/99bt56v4zs"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Alicia","last_name":"Zamudio","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Molecular Mechanism of Gene Regulation"],"journal_issn":[]}],"total_records":122158,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":90,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":122158,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":121698,"restricted":61,"embargoed":399},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","count":2061},{"id":"https://ror.org/003x0zc53","name":"Agencia Estatal de Investigación","count":1326},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","count":568},{"id":"https://ror.org/03ha2q922","name":"National Science Center","count":441},{"id":"https://ror.org/02ddkpn78","name":"Fundação de Amparo à Pesquisa do Estado de São Paulo","count":416},{"id":"https://ror.org/03swz6y49","name":"Conselho Nacional de Desenvolvimento Científico e Tecnológico","count":380},{"id":"https://ror.org/00x0ma614","name":"Coordenação de Aperfeicoamento de Pessoal de Nível Superior","count":379},{"id":"https://ror.org/021nxhr62","name":"U.S. National Science Foundation","count":337},{"id":"https://ror.org/018mejw64","name":"Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft","count":274},{"id":"https://ror.org/00hhkn466","name":"Japan Society for the Promotion of Science","count":247}],"source_stats":[{"id":"MENDELEY_DATA","name":"Mendeley Data","count":119590},{"id":"f881433a-b1cd-40d2-ab9f-4fd501f2d14b","name":"Elsevier Data Repository","count":1890},{"id":"a191461c-0227-403f-a1e9-621e0e0429cc","name":"San Raffaele Open Research Data Repository","count":174},{"id":"b07018a5-fee6-4df1-9d43-ff0d81ad75ed","name":"Bicocca Open Archive Research Data","count":127},{"id":"df228bbb-ac66-410f-8c66-f61608d63d95","name":"University of South Florida","count":70},{"id":"2a9d1b7d-e447-466f-888f-aa6f84e575e4","name":"Digital Commons Data@Becker","count":58},{"id":"72c28c45-627f-446b-8a48-af7c0a51a357","name":"Università Cattolica del Sacro Cuore","count":53},{"id":"b121dfec-bb51-4f19-a2f6-bc9ce0197286","name":"Babeș-Bolyai University","count":37},{"id":"7e51a2dd-133e-43c6-a220-12c1958e202a","name":"University of Canberra","count":30},{"id":"5f1edf21-7948-4d82-894f-c0ed4b1e8f52","name":"Teesside University","count":25}],"top_institutions_stats":[{"id":"ecb64011-71f0-5223-b4e4-4d27d5f51d83","name":"Universidad Autonoma de Madrid Centro de Biologia Molecular Severo Ochoa","count":866},{"id":"9d63ad1d-c391-5c35-a12e-cafe17017098","name":"Universidad Autonoma de Madrid","count":751},{"id":"0ad97c43-5173-56e6-bbe6-46dad5323862","name":"Daffodil International University","count":402},{"id":"3bfef41a-d03e-5722-bd55-dd245235fa78","name":"Bina Nusantara University","count":390},{"id":"f58d7a83-6efa-5d8a-a6c2-b9e78ee14783","name":"Maharishi Markandeshwar Institute of Physiotherapy and Rehabilitation","count":357}]}}